  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Moderate-to-strong fit: if the hashed CAS object is the full envelope, the in-band pCID binds payload interpretation to the bytes, so raw-vs-pointer meaning need not depend on filenames or local paths. But CID codec identity alone still does not answer the scenario; Bob must already know to parse this grid envelope and retain the referenced handler.",
    "strengths": [
      "When the full envelope is transported, pCID binds payload semantics to the bytes in-band.",
      "Unknown-pCID handling preserves opaque storage/forwarding while failing explicitly at the dispatch boundary.",
      "Mandatory signature over [pcid,payload] improves integrity and later auditability."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The outer bytes are only DAG-CBOR; CID codec identity alone does not mark them as this envelope.",
      "Opaque positional payloads are less self-describing for human or long-gap auditors.",
      "The variant is still draft and not yet frozen or minted as durable pCID material."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "If CAS names only inner payload bytes rather than the full envelope, object type binding disappears.",
      "Long-term interoperability depends on preserving pCID-to-spec/handler resolution.",
      "Malformed signatures or unknown signature protocols may fail inconsistently unless error handling is frozen."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Is the stable CAS hash meant to name the full envelope bytes or only the inner payload bytes?",
      "How does Bob recognize grid-envelope bytes versus arbitrary DAG-CBOR without extra context?",
      "Should raw chunks and pointer objects use distinct payload pCIDs or a dedicated CAS wrapper protocol?",
      "What freeze and minting path makes this variant durable enough for 100-year interoperability?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
